    <h1 class="rv in" style="font-size:clamp(2.2rem,5vw,4rem);max-width:20ch">Boat Trip or Levada Walk: Which Is Better With One Day in Madeira?</h1>
    <div class="artmeta rv in d1"><span>18 November 2026</span><span>5 min read</span><span>Funchal · Madeira</span></div>
  </div>
</header>

<section class="pad">
  <div class="wrap">
    <article class="article rv">
      <p class="lede">Let the weather choose: on a day with cloud on the mountains and a calm south coast, take the boat; on a day with clear peaks and a rough sea, walk a levada. If the forecast allows, an easy morning levada followed by the Sunset Trip in the evening gives you both in one day.</p>

      <h2>What does each one actually give you?</h2><p>A levada walk shows you the inside of Madeira: laurel forest, water channels cut along the hillsides, valleys that fall away into cloud. A boat trip shows you the outside: the cliffs, the coastal villages and the island from the water, where its height makes the most sense.</p><p>Neither replaces the other, which is why the choice is hard with only one day. The good news is that the island's weather often makes the decision for you.</p><h2>How do they compare, point by point?</h2><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Levada walk</th><th>Boat trip from Funchal</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Best weather</td><td>Clear mountains, dry paths</td><td>A calm sea on the south coast</td></tr><tr><td>Fitness</td><td>From easy and flat to a hard mountain day</td><td>Step on and off at the pontoon. Swimming is optional.</td></tr><tr><td>Time</td><td>Half a day to a full day, with the drive</td><td>2h to 3h, from the centre of Funchal</td></tr><tr><td>Booking</td><td>In 2026 classified trails need a booking with a time slot</td><td>Book the boat ahead</td></tr><tr><td>Cost</td><td>About 4.50 EUR per person over 12 on classified trails, plus transport</td><td>400, 500 or 600 EUR for the whole boat, up to 5 guests</td></tr><tr><td>Swimming</td><td>No</td><td>Yes on the Day Trip, no on the Sunset Trip</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Which is better in bad or mixed weather?</h2><p>It depends on which kind of bad. Madeira's weather changes with height and with the side of the island, so a grey morning in the mountains can be a fine morning on the water, and the other way round.</p><ul><li><strong>Cloud and drizzle up high, calm sea below.</strong> Take the boat. A levada in thick cloud means a wet path and no view.</li><li><strong>Clear sky, big swell.</strong> The sea is the problem, not the sky. If the skipper calls the trip off, you can rebook or take a full refund. Walk instead.</li><li><strong>Heavy rain.</strong> Neither is at its best. Levada paths get slippery and some trails close after storms. For the sea side, read <a href="/posts/what-happens-to-a-boat-trip-in-madeira-in-bad-weather">how bad weather affects a boat trip in Madeira</a>.</li></ul><p>In the mountains, clear views are more likely early in the morning. Cloud often builds over the high ground as the day goes on, which is why walkers start early and boat trips can wait until the afternoon or the evening.</p><h2>Which suits your fitness and your group?</h2><p>The boat asks less of your legs. The levada asks less of your stomach. That is the short version.</p><h3>Choose the levada if</h3><ul><li>you walk well and want to feel the island under your feet;</li><li>someone in the group gets seasick even on calm water;</li><li>your budget is tight and a short, easy trail is enough for you.</li></ul><h3>Choose the boat if</h3><ul><li>someone has bad knees, or the group mixes grandparents and teenagers;</li><li>you want to swim in the sea, not just look at it;</li><li>drops beside a narrow path make you uneasy. Some levadas have them, and vertigo is a real reason to skip a trail;</li><li>you want the day recorded without carrying a camera. On a Chifbay trip someone else does the filming: a 360° video, real camera photos and, weather allowing, aerial drone shots.</li></ul><h2>Which gives better light?</h2><p>It depends on the hour. In the mountains, the best light and the clearest views usually come early, before cloud builds. Inside laurel forest the light is soft and green at any time, which suits photos of the path more than wide views. On the water, the evening wins: the Sunset Trip gives you low sun on the cliffs, then the lights of Funchal coming on across the hills on the way back.</p><h2>Which levadas fit into a day with a boat trip?</h2><p>The short ones. Two official trails show the range:</p><ul><li><strong>PR 11 Vereda dos Balcões</strong>, from Ribeiro Frio: 1.5 km each way, 3 km in total, flat and easy, about 1 h 30 on the official listing. It ends at a viewpoint over deep green valleys towards the central peaks. It fits a morning before a Sunset Trip, and our <a href="/posts/ribeiro-frio-balcoes-madeira-guide">Ribeiro Frio and Balcões guide</a> has the details.</li><li><strong>PR 6 Levada das 25 Fontes</strong>, from Rabaçal: 4.3 km each way, 8.6 km in total, rated moderate, about 3 hours on the official listing and often longer once you add the walk from the car park and the Risco waterfall detour. With the drive, that is a full morning, so it belongs on a day of its own.</li></ul><p>In 2026 both trails need a booking and payment on the regional SIMplifica portal before you walk, at about 4.50 EUR per person over 12. Book the slot as soon as you know the day. Our <a href="/posts/levada-walks-madeira-guide">levada walks guide</a> covers fees, rules and the main trails.</p><h2>Can you do both, in one day or in two?</h2><p>Yes, if you put the walk first and the sea last.</p><h3>One day</h3><ol><li>Leave Funchal early for Ribeiro Frio and walk the Balcões trail while the mountains are clear.</li><li>Come back to Funchal for lunch and a rest.</li><li>Take the <a href="/sunset-cruise">Sunset Trip</a>, which leaves 1 h 15 before sunset: around 16:45 in December, around 20:00 in June. There is no swimming on it, so tired legs do not matter.</li></ol><h3>Two days</h3><ol><li>Day one: the <a href="/hidden-coves-half-day">Day Trip at 10:00</a>, two swims on the coast west of Funchal, and the afternoon left free.</li><li>Day two: an early start for 25 Fontes or another longer levada.</li><li>If the sea forecast looks bad for day one, swap the days.</li></ol><p>The swap is what makes two days so much better than one. You give each activity its best weather instead of taking whatever the single day brings. Chifbay's free cancellation up to 24 hours before departure makes that swap simple to arrange.</p><h2>Is there a day for neither?</h2><p>Sometimes, yes. If you have only one day, have just arrived on an early flight and slept badly, a long mountain drive plus a walk is a poor choice, and so is a fast boat trip in a swell. On that kind of day, walk Funchal's seafront, look at both trips on our <a href="/experiences">experiences page</a> and book the one the next forecast favours.</p><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>Is there an easy levada for a short morning?</h3><p>Yes. PR 11 Vereda dos Balcões at Ribeiro Frio is 3 km in total, flat and easy, and takes about 1 h 30 on the official listing.</p><h3>Do you have to pay to walk a levada in Madeira?</h3><p>In 2026 classified trails cost about 4.50 EUR per person over 12, booked in advance on the SIMplifica portal. Children up to 12 walk free.</p><h3>Can you swim on a levada walk?</h3><p>No. If swimming in the sea matters to you, the Day Trip has two swim stops, at Fajã dos Padres and at Ribeira Brava.</p><h3>What if both the sea and the mountains look bad?</h3><p>Stay low and stay flexible. Spend the day in Funchal and move the boat or the walk to the next good forecast.</p>

      <blockquote>Walk where the cloud is not, sail where the swell is not.</blockquote>
    </article>
    <p class="pref-src" data-pref-source><span>Enjoying our Madeira guides?</span> <a href="https://www.google.com/preferences/source?q=chifbay.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="18" height="18" aria-hidden="true"><path fill="#4285F4" d="M23.5 12.3c0-.8-.1-1.6-.2-2.3H12v4.5h6.5a5.6 5.6 0 0 1-2.4 3.6v3h3.9c2.2-2.1 3.5-5.1 3.5-8.8z"/><path fill="#34A853" d="M12 24c3.2 0 6-1.1 7.9-2.9l-3.9-3c-1.1.7-2.4 1.2-4 1.2-3.1 0-5.7-2.1-6.6-4.9h-4v3.1A12 12 0 0 0 12 24z"/><path fill="#FBBC05" d="M5.4 14.4a7.2 7.2 0 0 1 0-4.7V6.6h-4a12 12 0 0 0 0 10.8l4-3z"/><path fill="#EA4335" d="M12 4.8c1.7 0 3.3.6 4.6 1.8l3.4-3.4A12 12 0 0 0 1.4 6.6l4 3.1C6.3 6.9 8.9 4.8 12 4.8z"/></svg>Add Chifbay as a preferred source on Google</a></p>

